Usage: gskill upgrade [<skill> ...] [flags]

Move a skill's declared version in skills.toml, then resolve, lock, install,
and verify it.

upgrade changes what skills.toml declares, then resolves, locks, installs, and
verifies the result. update never edits skills.toml; upgrade is how a declared
version moves.

Examples:

    gskill upgrade my-skill
    gskill upgrade my-skill --latest
    gskill upgrade my-skill 2.1.0
    gskill upgrade my-skill --to 2.1.0
    gskill --no-interactive upgrade --all
    gskill --dry-run upgrade my-skill

Arguments:
  [<skill> ...]    Skills to upgrade. With exactly one skill, a second argument
                   is the target version (same as --to).

Flags:
  -h, --help              Show context-sensitive help.
      --json              Emit machine-readable JSON on stdout.
      --quiet             Suppress diagnostics on stderr.
      --no-interactive    Disable prompts and colored output.
      --yes               Assume yes for confirmation prompts.
      --offline           Operate without network access.
      --no-cache          Bypass the content cache.
      --dry-run           Report actions without applying them.
      --config=STRING     Path to a TOML config file; overrides the user config
                          file. Must exist.
  -v, --verbose           Enable verbose diagnostics.
  -C, --dir=STRING        Run as if gskill started in this directory.

      --to=STRING         Exact target version or tag; must exist as a release
                          of the skill's source.
      --latest            Move to the newest stable release beyond the current
                          declaration (the default).
      --all               Without skill names and outside a terminal: upgrade
                          every upgradable skill.
